# dataset.yaml.example - head detection project
# Author: Sathish (sathish.c@magellanic-cloud.com) — Magellanic Cloud, TrainX
#
# REFERENCE ONLY. Do NOT ship a live dataset.yaml:
# the pipeline GENERATES the real one per run at
#   /trainx/workspaces/<project>/<dataset_id>/dataset.yaml
# and passes it to train.py via --data. For this project it looks like this:
#
# train: /trainx/workspaces/<project>/<dataset_id>/train_split.txt   # newline-delimited absolute image paths
# val:   /trainx/workspaces/<project>/<dataset_id>/val_split.txt
# nc: 1                                  # regenerated from CVAT labels EVERY run
# names: ["head"]                        # never hardcode a class list in the repo
#
# THE ORDER AND SPELLING OF `names` ARE NOT OURS TO CHOOSE. They come from the
# project's CVAT label list, so class id 0 is whatever CVAT says, and the
# label may be 'head', 'Head', 'person_head' or anything else the annotation
# team agreed on. Nothing in this repo may assume "head is id 0":
#   * train.py reads nc/names from this file to REPORT them (preflight) and to
#     recognize WHICH label is the head class - never to assume an id
#   * export.py copies them into model_package.json in that same id order
#   * deploy/edge_client.py resolves 'head' BY NAME through that list
# Reorder or rename the labels in CVAT and the whole chain still works.
#
# A second class is fine too (e.g. names: ["head", "person"]) - the pipeline
# regenerates nc/names and the preflight reports both. The head-specific
# checks simply apply to whichever label is recognized as the head class.
#
# Labels resolve the standard YOLOv5 way: each image path's images/ segment is
# replaced by labels/ and .txt appended (one YOLO-format line per box), e.g.
#   .../versions/<id>/images/frame_00042.jpg
#   .../versions/<id>/labels/frame_00042.txt
#     0 0.512 0.184 0.041 0.052     <- head (note how SMALL a head box is:
#     0 0.333 0.201 0.038 0.047        ~4-5% of frame height is typical, which
#     0 0.774 0.167 0.029 0.036        is why --img 640 is the floor here)
#
# Annotate EVERY head in the frame, including the small ones at the back. A
# half-annotated crowd teaches the model that distant heads are background -
# precisely the recall this project needs most.
#
# A frame with no people at all is a legitimate background image and gets an
# EMPTY .txt (not a missing one). Those are worth having: headrests, posters
# of faces, mannequins and lamps are what a head detector false-positives on.
